This began shortly after Jeremy became a leader, and my colleagues, for whom I previously thought to share similar political perspectives, expected me to change my mind and take positions with which I would otherwise never agree – be it in the field of national security or the EU's single market.
Speaking publicly – and it doesn’t really matter what I’m supposed to say—stracks criticism on social media calling for non-selebring, judging the policy of the mid-wife and telling me that it shouldn’t be in the Labour Party.
And it's not just my experience.
In fact, I know that I am more fortunate than some of my counterparts, because the comments intended for me are only political.
Admire the professionalism and determination of those colleagues who face a flood of sexist or racist insults on a daily basis, but do not allow themselves to be frightened.
One of the most reluctant aspects of this period of politics is how abuse rates have become quite common.
Jeremy Corbyn explained last week that the Labour Party must promote a culture of cesspoism.
However, the reality is that we are no longer open to diligent opinions, and with every motion of censure no-confessor or a revision of the rules for choosing a party, it is becoming more narrow-minded.
In the last two years, I have received a lot of advice to bow my head and not be so loud, and I’ll “everyone in order”.
I didn’t come into politics because of it.
Since I’ve joined the Labour Party as a pupil of 32 years ago—the reason for this was the negligence of Tatcher’s government, which literally collapsed in my elementary school – as a local councillor or government minister, I tried to promote better public services for those most in need.
I've never miscarried my political beliefs, including the last elections.
No one in the Nottingham East could be confused by my political views and areas I did not agree with the current leadership.
I would just like to say to those who supported the proposal on Friday that I don't understand their desire to waste time and energy to discuss my fiasure to the leader of the Labour Party at a time when the state is sailing against Brexit, which will hurt households, companies and our public services.
But my true message is not for Nottingham Momentum, but for my constituents, regardless of whether they are members of the Labour Party or not: I am proud that I have served you, and I promise you that no threat of non-selection or politience will discourage me from acting in the best interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is a member of the Nottingham East Parliament
