Scottish solitary schools enjoy the reputation of academic Excellence and continued in 2018 with another set of outstanding results in exams, which are further strengthened by individual and collective successes in sport, art, music and other activities of the community.
With more than 30,000 learners across Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Independent Schools (SCISs), they strive to provide the highest level of service for their students and their parents.
Semi-si-tuisterial schools want to prepare their pupils for further and higher education, the chosen career path and the place they hold as global citizens.
As an education sector that can design and implement a personalized school program, we see that modern languages are still a popular and desired elective course in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you talk to a man in a language he understands, the conversation is imitated.
“But if you’re talking to a man in his mother tongue, the words are imitated in his heart.”
It’s a good reminder that we shouldn’t simply rely on English when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
Receipt reviews of recent exams show that languages are at the forefront of the list with the highest success rates in independent schools.
68% of students who learned a foreign language achieved an excellent grade.
Data collected in the 74 schools of the members of the Scottish Independent Schools (SCIS), showed that 72% of the students achieved an excellent grade in Mandarin, and 72% of German pupils, 69% of French pupils and 63 percent of Spanish pupils received an excellent grade.
This proves that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as a key knowledge that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
As an electitiable course in the scheduling plans of independent schools and elsewhere, languages are treated in the same way as subjects in the fields of natural sciences, technology, engineering and mathematics.
A survey carried out in 2014 by the UK Employment and Training Commission found that the reasons why employers are tormented to fill their vacancies can be attributable to a lack of language proficiency.
As a result, the knowledge of languages is becoming more and more necessary in the preparation of young people for their future career.
Future job prospects that require language proficiency, these skills are of paramount importance in the global world.
If someone learns a different language, regardless of the professional path he chooses with such a life-long knowledge, has a great advantage in the future.
A multi-language person who can directly interact with people from foreign countries automatically takes precedence over the competition.
A youGov poll, covering more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom, found that 75 per cent of people didn’t speak a foreign language well enough to be able to talk, with French being the only language spoken by a two-digit percentage of respondents, i.e. 15 per cent.
Therefore, investing in language learning is important for today's children.
With the knowledge of several languages, especially those from countries with a developing economy, children have a better chance to find a suitable job.
In Scotland, schools differ in the languages they teach.
Numerous schools focus on more classical modern languages, while others teach languages that are supposed to be most relevant to the UK in 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Irresistally on the interests of your child, it will always be possible to choose a number of languages taught by specialists in this field in independent schools.
Scottish independent schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare and arm children with the knowledge they need to succeed, no matter what the future brings.
In the global business environment, it is impossible to deny that languages remain extremely important for the future of the country, so this should also be expressed in education.
In Fact, modern languages should be regarded as “international communication skills”.
Semi-si-tuisterial schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence for young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
