HIV/aids: 14% increase in new cases reported from China
China has announced an 14% increase in citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
Medical officials say more than 820,000 people are affected.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported in the second-a quarter of 2018.
In the vast majority of new cases, the disease was transmitted through sexual intercourse, which represents a change compared to the past.
Typically, HIV spread rapidly in some parts of China due to a transfusion of infected womb.
However, according to Chinese medical officials from the Junan conference in the Junan province, the number of people infected with HIV in this way was reduced to almost zero.
However, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS is still increasing annually by 100,000 people in China.
Transmitting HIV with sexual intercourse is a very pressing problem in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discriminated against people from the LGBT community is still highly present.
Since conservative values are still valid in the country, according to the results of surveys, 70–90% of men who have sex with men eventually marry a woman.
The disease is often tolerated due to inappropriate protection in sexual intercourse in such relationships.
Since 2003, as part of its efforts to solve the problem, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to medicines for the treatment of HIV.
