The Global Citizens Festival, launched in 2012, is an extension of the Global Citizens Project, an international advocacy group that hopes to eradicate Poverty by elevating the number of people who are actively fighting it.
In order to receive a vacant ticket to the event (unless you were willing to pay for a VIP ticket), the participants of the Concert had to perform a series of tasks or “actions”, such as volunteering, a letter to a global leader, a phone call or any other sensible way to raise awareness of their goal of retaliation.
How successfully is the Global Citizen movement, which has 12 years left to achieve its goal?
Is the idea of repricing people with a free concert the right way to persuade them to take action, or is it just another example of the so-called “clictivism”—creating a belief that people actually have a significant impact on the situation by signing an online petition or forwarding a twit?
