Red Tide Newsletter: Concentrations along Pinellas, Manete and Saratos districts are declining
The most recent report by the Florid Commission on the Protection of Fish and Rail (FWC) demonstrates an overall decrease in red tide concentrations in parts of Tampa Bay area.
FWC has reported more diffused flowering in the areas of Pinellas, Manee, Saratos, Charlotte and Collier districts, indicating a decrease in concentrations.
The red tide stretches about 130 miles from the north coast of the Penellas districts to the southern districts of Lee.
Stairs look about $10 mile from Hillsborough coast, but in fewer places than last week.
A red tide has also been spotted in Pasco County.
Over the course of the past week, there have been reports of midconcentrations in Pinlolas and along its coast, low to high concentrations off the coast of Hillsborough district, basic upheavals in Manetee district, basic to high-ranking Concentrations in Saratos County and along its coast, basic to middle-concentrations in Charlotte County, basic to high-rise concentrations in Lee County and along its coast, and low concentrations in Collier County.
Respiratory Irritation continues to be reported from the counties of Pinellas, Mantee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Respiratory Irritation in the past week has not been reported from Northwestern Florida.
